0 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050240 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050240 #edb/ref 1031 "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050240 #edb/ref 1033 493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050240 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "38bc1722-543b-58cb-b448-fb93b0e623de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050981 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186192817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050981 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186192818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050981 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186192819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050981 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186192820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050981 #edb/ref 1031 "Absolute Value Equations With Extraneous Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050981 #edb/ref 1033 3992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050981 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "76cbf6b4-b13e-5992-82a1-6e1f2332f7a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110368 #edb/ref 1069 "e-17212" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110368 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(5s + 4)x = 9x\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110368 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a8f8deb-2ff1-56d2-888a-3e51eb805bc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110368 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 5s + 4, \\qquad b = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5s + 4 = 9.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5s & =5 \\\\ s & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110371 #edb/ref 1069 "e-18852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110371 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110371 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6eeec12a-5da8-5113-916a-58f4ca807207" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110371 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n3r = 12.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k = 2$ and $r = 4,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + r = 2+4 = 6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110372 #edb/ref 1069 "e-18854" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110372 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$\n\n- $-3x = -4x + 6$\n\n- $2x + 7 = 2x - 1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110372 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6355ac1e-bb4d-5133-9917-04dfb2bc5a0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110372 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =4x+1 \\\\ 4x+1-4x & =4x+1-4x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x & =-4x+6 \\\\ -3x+4x & =-4x+6+4x \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-3x = -4x + 6$ has the unique solution $x = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+7 & =2x-1 \\\\ 2x+7-2x & =2x-1-2x \\\\ 7 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110719 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110719 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110719 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110719 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186476103 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186110719 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110719 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110720 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110720 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110720 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110720 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110720 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110720 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476179 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186110720 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476183 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186110720 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476185 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186110720 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476189 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186110720 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476193 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186110720 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving For an Unknown Parameter" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218611072