edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1045 "Linear Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f14be6fb-7490-5e50-9e2d-7ccd72daa91c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111678 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111678 #edb/ref 1061 476188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111678 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eab36dd4-7684-5fb5-a213-f02f98e0da39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111678 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111679 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111679 #edb/ref 1061 476189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111679 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "310129a9-81a2-54d5-b227-633c2cb1b8d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111679 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111680 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111680 #edb/ref 1061 476192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111680 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "888ce267-7f48-5074-a1ab-42caf954a38c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111680 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111681 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111681 #edb/ref 1061 476193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111681 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f94c8cb-7345-56bc-b884-f0ac3f242910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111681 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111682 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111682 #edb/ref 1061 476194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111682 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1e04eac3-4a93-5d72-9a90-2b1099628885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111682 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111683 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111683 #edb/ref 1061 476197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111683 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d109dfd-fdee-5718-a3d2-a9a96f41ffd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111683 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111684 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111684 #edb/ref 1061 476198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111684 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01172b4e-9bb6-512f-9e14-1b2ad76e2a6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111684 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111685 #edb/ref 1061 476199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111685 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c8fdb7d5-63c2-55ac-a293-d9536babfe47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111685 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111814 #edb/ref 1064 "Some linear equations have **infinitely many solutions.** \n\nFor example, consider the following equation: \n\n$$\nx+5 = x + 5\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation by subtracting $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ x+5-x & =x+5-x \\\\ 5 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, what does $x$ equal? The statement $5=5$ is *always* true, irrespective of the value of $x.$ This tells us that *any* value of $x$ is a solution to the equation!\n\nDon't believe it? If we substitute any value, say $x=3,$ into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ 3+5 & =3+5 \\\\ 8 & =8.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's important to note that substituting *any* value for $x$ into the equation will lead to a true statement. For example, the values \n\n$$\nx=42,\\qquad x=-\\dfrac 1 4, \\qquad x=1.2345\n$$\n\nare *all* solutions to the original equation.\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- Our original equation has *infinitely many solutions.*\n\n- The solution of the equation is \"*any (real) value of* $x$\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111814 #edb/ref 1065 11389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111814 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "edb6ae26-e191-5d7d-9ce8-ad9ee36655f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111814 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111815 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2qx = 10x,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. If we're given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{10}_{\\large b} \\ x\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n$$\na = 2q, \\qquad b = 10.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b.$ Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2q = 10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q & =10 \\\\ \\frac{2q}{2} & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2qx = 10x$ has infinitely many solutions if $q = 5.$\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by substituting $q = 5$ into our original equation. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2qx & =10x \\\\ 2(5)x & =10x \\\\ 10x & =10x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand simplifying this equation by subtracting $10x$ from both sides yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x & =10x \\\\ 10x-10x & =10x-10x \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $x.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111815 #edb/ref 1065 11486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111815 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0d28dc5e-68de-53d0-a57c-9072a89eccbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111815 #edb/ref 1067 "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111817 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(s - 2)x + t = 6x + 9,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the values of $s$ and $t?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax+b = cx+d{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(s-2)}_{\\large a} \\ x + \\underbrace{t}_{\\large b} = \\underbrace{6}_{\\large c} \\ x + \\underbrace{9}_{\\large d}\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax+b = cx+d$ has infinitely many solutions when $a=c$ and $b=d.$\n\nSo, to determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}})x + {\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}x + {\\color{red}{{9}}}\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we **equate the coefficients** of $x,$ and we **equate the constants:**\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{red}{{9}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $s = 8$ and $t=9,$ our original equation has infinitely many solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111817 #edb/ref 1065 13403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111817 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dc5c5f3e-0731-5ab3-8afd-a2cf24228db1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111817 #edb/ref 1067 "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters in General Linear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111818 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nFor example, each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x=2x,\\,\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{1}{3}x\\,-2.16x=-2.16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo show that the general equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ we can substitute $a$ for $b$ in our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =bx \\\\ ax & =(a)x \\\\ ax & =ax\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =ax \\\\ ax-ax & =ax-ax \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation, $0=0,$ is always true, regardless of the value of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ and any value of $x$ is a solution to the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111818 #edb/ref 1065 13429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111818 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8428afc7-2e9a-55d0-9f13-7a2acdf8fd7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111818 #edb/ref 1067 "Conditions for an Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111819 #edb/ref 1064 "Each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n2x + 1 = 2x + 1, \\qquad \\dfrac13x -2 = \\dfrac13x -2, \\qquad 1-x = 1 - x\n$$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. Then, this equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nNote that if we substitute $c=a$ and $d=b$ into this equation, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =cx+d \\\\ ax+b & =(a)x+(b) \\\\ ax+b & =ax+b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =ax+b \\\\ ax+b-ax & =ax+b-ax \\\\ b & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation, $b = b,$ is always true. Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions, and any real value of $x$ is a solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111819 #edb/ref 1065 13430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111819 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7498a4f8-36b5-5d1d-b953-893581bfa9aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111819 #edb/ref 1067 "Conditi